<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The app's hardened-runtime entitlements — deliberately EMPTY, and that is the
  finding, not an oversight.

  Under `codesign --options runtime` an entitlement is an EXCEPTION to a
  restriction, requested only where the app would otherwise be denied.
  packages/mcp-eventkit's helper needs
  com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars because without it tccd
  refuses to even prompt (docs/ops/apple-signing.md §3). This app needs none of
  that:

  - It touches no TCC-protected resource itself. Calendars and Reminders reach
    the system through the EventKit helper, which is its own signed bundle with
    its own grant (PoC-1: a stdio child inherits the parent's TCC identity, so
    TCC work never happens in the parent).
  - Spawning `metistry` is not restricted by the hardened runtime; only loading
    code into THIS process is, and the one framework it loads —
    Sparkle.framework — is signed with the same identity, so library validation
    is satisfied and com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation is not
    needed.
  - com.apple.security.app-sandbox stays absent. The app drives an installer:
    it writes launchd plists into ~/Library/LaunchAgents through `metistry up`
    and creates an instance repo wherever the user points it. (The sandbox
    profile the plan does call for is for the ASSISTANT process, not this one —
    docs/product/desktop-app-plan.md, decision #15.)

  Keep this file even while it is empty: the next person to reach for an
  entitlement should find the reasoning, not a blank. `codesign` hands this to
  AMFI, whose XML parser rejects comments, so build-app.sh runs it through
  `plutil -convert xml1` into a temp file before signing — the same trick the
  helper build scripts use to keep the *why* in the checked-in file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
